KW  - Dynamic reliability
KW  - Decision support systems
KW  - Uncertainty management
KW  - Human-machine interaction
KW  - Rainwater harvesting systems
KW  - HARVESTING SYSTEMS
KW  - WATER-TREATMENT
KW  - PARTICLE DETACHMENT
KW  - RAINFALL
KW  - FILTRATION
KW  - QUALITY
KW  - ARIMA
KW  - OPERATION
KW  - SECURITY
KW  - DESIGN
AB  - In the context of climate change, robust operational strategies are required to mitigate water supply depletion and ensure the reliable operation of decentralized water infrastructure systems. Rainwater Harvesting, Treatment and Reuse Systems (RHTRSs) are a feasible option, especially for non-drinking purposes, but their effectiveness depends on maintaining service continuity and compliance with water quality standards under uncertain environmental and operating conditions. In this study, a reliability-oriented Digital Twin (DT) was developed and implemented to simulate the RHTRS of a commercial building, combining the Building Information Modeling (BIM)-based geometry, hydrological study, physical-chemical treatment, Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA) modeling, and an immersive Mixed Reality (MR) interface. The DT enables dynamic reliability assessment under uncertainty of system operation under stochastic environmental and demand variability by coupling structural data, hydraulic behavior, and time-series predictions of critical variables, including water availability and total suspended solids. The joint application of BIM, DT, ARIMA-based forecasting module supporting future soft sensing (e.g., turbidimeter), and MR visualization of a real-world, building-scale RHTRS is novel and provides a decision-support system to enhance the reliability, resilience, and safety of complex constructed infrastructure.
