TI  - Enhancing safety in construction: A framework predicting neuro-cognitive tipping points from environmental stressors
T2  - SAFETY SCIENCE
LA  - English
KW  - Construction safety
KW  - Neuro-cognitive modeling
KW  - Cognitive tipping points
KW  - Environmental stressors
KW  - Human factors
KW  - Neuroergonomics
KW  - WORKING-MEMORY CAPACITY
KW  - PREFRONTAL CORTEX
KW  - LOAD THEORY
KW  - AMYGDALA
KW  - HIPPOCAMPAL
KW  - PERFORMANCE
KW  - REPRESENTATION
KW  - INTEGRATION
KW  - ACCIDENTS
KW  - CLIMATE
AB  - Current construction safety protocols often stagnate by treating human error as a stochastic variable rather than a predictable, mechanistically driven parameter. Addressing this critical gap, this study introduces the Neuro Cognitive Safety Framework (NCSF), a novel interdisciplinary paradigm that bridges environmental multiphysics stressors with worker neurobiology. Anchored by a Traceability Dictionary of over 150 empirically validated concepts and a hierarchical Level of Detail (LOD) architecture, the NCSF operationalizes Cognitive Load Theory. The framework's core mechanistic concepts were empirically validated using an experimental Electroencephalography (EEG) and n-back dataset (N = 16). Results demonstrated significant behavioral degradation under peak cognitive load (accuracy dropping to 69.6%), which was preceded by predictive neurospectral markers, including parietal theta (similar to 4-6 Hz) enhancement and frontal alpha (similar to 9-11 Hz) suppression (p < 0.05). Building upon this empirical foundation, a predictive Agent-Based Model (ABM) was developed to simulate how environmental stressors, specifically thermal and acoustic loads, non-linearly accelerate "Neural Resource Competition." The simulation successfully identified a critical acoustic "Cognitive Tipping Point" around 73 dB. Crucially, rather than assuming a uniform group limit, the model captured profound interindividual variability. Beyond these personalized thresholds, prefrontal executive control is depleted, predisposing workers to a "Habit Switch" where they regress to amygdala-driven reflexive reactions. Ultimately, by translating these specific neuro-cognitive parameters into a predictive computational domain, the NCSF provides the foundational logic for Industry 5.0 applications, such as Neuro-Cognitive Digital Twins (NCDTs), enabling a paradigm shift from reactive compliance to proactive, individualized safety engineering.
