TI  - Cross-domain adaptive digital twin modelling for dam structures using graph attention networks
T2  - ADVANCED ENGINEERING INFORMATICS
LA  - English
KW  - Dam safety monitoring
KW  - Digital twin
KW  - Deep learning
KW  - Transfer learning
KW  - Finite element method
KW  - Domain adaptation
AB  - Digital twin technology has emerged as a promising paradigm for advancing instrumentation and measurement in dam safety monitoring. However, existing models often face significant cross-domain discrepancies between numerical simulations (source domain) and in-situ measurement data (target domain), which constrain prediction accuracy and generalization under varying operating conditions. To address these challenges, this study proposes a novel cross-domain adaptive digital twin framework that integrates graph attention networks, gating mechanisms, and feature alignment strategies to enhance measurement consistency and predictive reliability. Multi-source numerical simulation domains are systematically constructed to enrich input variability and improve measurement-domain coverage. The proposed framework is comprehensively validated through ablation studies and comparisons with six state-of-the-art baseline models, achieving superior accuracy with mean errors as low as 0.44 mm and 0.99 %, thereby demonstrating excellent measurement precision. Furthermore, validation under extreme water-level and temperature scenarios confirms strong generalization, with the inclusion of extreme-condition samples further reducing errors and mitigating prediction degradation at critical monitoring points. The results show that the proposed approach provides a physically consistent, generalizable, and robust digital twin measurement framework for dam structures, enabling reliable real-time deformation monitoring and safety evaluation. These findings establish a foundation for intelligent, large-scale, and multi-condition applications of digital twins in civil infrastructure monitoring and measurement science.
