AB  - Cable-supported bridges are critical civil infrastructure. With increasing traffic volume and extreme weather conditions, these bridges face threats to their functional and structural safety during their service life, necessitating timely health monitoring and condition assessment. In recent years, digital twin (DT) has achieved significant progress in bridge monitoring and assessment, effectively addressing the shortcomings of traditional methods. However, current bridge DTs, especially those for cable-supported bridges, still face a series of issues, such as ambiguous concepts and lack of unified standards. To enrich the discussion on this topic, this paper explores the current research trends and frameworks of bridge DT through bibliometric analysis and a comprehensive review of the latest advancements, providing a systematic overview of relevant research on health monitoring and condition assessment of cable-supported bridges based on DT. Firstly, the origin, definition, and characteristics of DT are introduced. Secondly, a systematic literature review is employed to statistically analyze the current research status and discuss the construction methods of the DT system. Subsequently, a novel three-layer framework for DT is proposed, which can be applied to health monitoring and condition assessment of cable-supported bridges. Finally, the challenges and future trends of DT in this field are discussed. The challenges consist of seven aspects, which are data management, modeling, security and privacy, service applications, intelligence, integration, and standardization. Future DT development will focus on advancing digitization, integration, and intelligence, while deepening theoretical research and practical applications. The establishment of a reliable operational condition assessment framework is also an important trend.
