TI  - Digital twins for low-altitude transportation and logistics: A barrier-centric survey of UAV and eVTOL systems
T2  - TRANSPORTATION RESEARCH PART E-LOGISTICS AND TRANSPORTATION REVIEW
LA  - English
KW  - Digital twin
KW  - UAV
KW  - Evtol
KW  - Low-altitude transportation
KW  - Low-altitude logistics
KW  - Traffic management
KW  - RESOURCE
AB  - Unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) and electric vertical takeoff and landing (eVTOL) platforms are increasingly considered for urban air mobility (UAM), last-mile logistics, and emergency-response operations, yet large-scale low-altitude deployment remains limited. Digital twin (DT) technology supports real-time monitoring, prediction, and control, while existing studies provide limited explanation for why some low-altitude DT application domains advance toward deployment more readily than other domains do. This survey interprets application maturity through a barriercentric framework that combines a four-layer architecture, feedback-loop timing distinctions, and an application taxonomy organized around three recurring constraints: the Fidelity-Latency Paradox, Information-Theoretic Limits, and the artificial intelligence (AI) Verification Challenge. Scenario comparison across urban UAM, rural or peri-urban logistics, and emergency response shows that the same DT architecture can imply different deployment boundaries depending on operational role, feedback timing, and non-technical conditions. Across applications, predictive maintenance and diagnostic functions appear comparatively mature, whereas path planning, traffic management, and safety-critical real-time control remain more constrained by computational, communication, and assurance requirements. For transportation and logistics research, the main implication is that DT deployment should be assessed by how computational limits, communication capacity, and certification requirements interact with regulation, infrastructure, and business conditions.
