TY  - JOUR
AU  - Van Tam, N
TI  - Autonomy is not enough: why agentic AI in the built environment demands governance
T2  - ENERGY AND BUILDINGS
LA  - English
KW  - Agentic AI
KW  - Built environment
KW  - Responsible autonomy
KW  - AI governance
KW  - Risk-adaptive governance
AB  - Agentic artificial intelligence (AI), defined as AI systems capable of autonomous perception, reasoning, planning, and action execution, signals a decisive shift in how the built environment is operated, managed, and optimized. However, increasing autonomy alone does not guarantee safe, legitimate, or socially acceptable outcomes in long-lived, safety-critical, and occupant-centered socio-technical systems. This paper advances a clear argument structured around three interlinked dimensions: promise, peril, and governed autonomy. Firstly, it synthesizes emerging empirical evidence demonstrating the promise of agentic AI in autonomous HVAC control and building energy management, agentic fault detection and diagnosis, intelligent digital twins, indoor environmental quality, and autonomous virtual building modelling. Secondly, it examines the perils associated with increasing AI autonomy, distinguishing between traditional automation risks amplified by agentic systems and novel agentic risks, including goal drift, hallucination, emergent behaviour, accountability gaps, explainability limitations, privacy concerns, and agency displacement. Thirdly, this paper argues that the central challenge is not the technical feasibility of agentic AI but the governance of autonomy itself. To address this challenge, it proposes responsible autonomy pathways based on risk-adaptive governance, governance-by-design through explainable architectures, alignment with emerging AI governance frameworks (i.e., ISO/IEC 42001), participatory governance models, and incremental deployment through regulatory sandboxes and living laboratories. The central argument is that responsible autonomy, rather than unrestricted autonomy, is essential for translating agentic AI's technical capabilities into trustworthy, sustainable, and socially beneficial outcomes in the built environment.
