AB  - Compounding weather hazards, such as wildfire-induced outages and urban heatwaves, challenge the reliability, affordability, and safety of urban services. We present a Hazard-Responsive Digital Twin (H-RDT) that combines physics-informed neural network modeling, multimodal data fusion, affiliation learning, and affordability-aware risk analytics for urban-scale response. In a screening-level synthetic district with diverse building archetypes and populations, a simulated wildfire-outage-heatwave cascade shows that H-RDT maintains stable indoor temperature predictions (similar to 31-33 degrees C peaks) under partial sensor loss, while reproducing outage-driven surges and recovery. The reinforcement learning based fusion module adaptively reweights IoT, UAV, and satellite inputs to sustain spatiotemporal coverage, and the affordability-adjusted mapping isolates high-vulnerability clusters such as schools, clinics, low-income housing. Prospective interventions, i.e., preemptive cooling-center activation and microgrid sharing, reduce population-weighted thermal risk by 11-13%, shrink the 95th-percentile (tail) risk by 7-17%, and cut overheating hours by up to 9%. Beyond this synthetic demonstration, the framework establishes a transferable pathway for real-city implementation by linking hazard modeling, reliability-focused analytics, and decision intelligence for resilience planning.
