KW  - embodied AI
KW  - few-shot in-context learning
KW  - industry 4.0
KW  - large language model
KW  - prompt engineering
KW  - PRINTED CONCRETE
KW  - CONSTRUCTION
KW  - PERFORMANCE
KW  - FABRICATION
KW  - PRODUCT
AB  - The industrialisation of 3D concrete printing (3DCP) is impeded by the lack of universal standards, a situation exacerbated by the process's sensitivity to multiple interdependent parameters and environmental conditions, which can introduce structural defects and performance inconsistencies. Starting from the definition of 3DCP as a cyberphysical production system (CPPS), this exploratory study proposes adopting a hybrid standardisation method that optimises structural performance, or concrete grade. By following the DSR/ASTM V&V research framework and based on the preliminary characterisation of materials, a computational digital twin is implemented, including a compressive strength predictive model connected to the simulation of the adaptive 3DCP process. Accounting for the anisotropic behaviour of 3D-printed concrete (3DPC), the predictive model determines the 3DPC grade based on preliminary material characterisation results and considering different process factors that determine the mechanical performance: By optimising process parameters, the model maximises parallel compression strength, prioritising safety. Uncertainty propagation is embedded in the computational model, enabling statistical validation through extensive virtual testing. The optimised configuration of process parameters will serve as a benchmark for the adaptive 3DCP process, in which a pretrained large language model acts as a high-level orchestrator for closed-loop control via few-shot in-context learning. The proposed architecture relies on the performance predictive model, sensor readings, and an LLM that interprets the process status and suggests corrective actions. The primary novel contribution of this work is a computational performance predictive model with uncertainty propagation; the adaptive 3DCP is described as completing the digital twin definition and demonstrating its use in a production setting. Starting from preliminary materials characterisation and considering the specificities of our robotic cell and environmental conditions, the performance predictive model reduced the variance in parallel structural strength by 25%, ensuring a 90% confidence level in achieving the M20 grade, with a significant reduction in anisotropic behaviour. Additionally, while maximising 3DPC performance, the digital twin enables benchmarking of production parameters across any production setting, making it a valid tool for standardising the technology. This research proposes a critical transition from deterministic to flexible standardisation, and from training-for-control to a more versatile prompting-for-control AI methodology, offering a viable, data-efficient pathway towards standardised, reliable, and industrial-scale 3DCP.
