TI  - Artificial intelligence-based tunnel leakage detection and monitoring: A review of sensing technologies and multimodal fusion
T2  - MEASUREMENT
LA  - English
KW  - Tunnel leakage
KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - Sensing
KW  - Multimodal fusion
KW  - Measurement uncertainty
KW  - Sensor calibration
KW  - Pluralistic resonant perception
KW  - MICROSEISMIC SOURCE LOCATION
KW  - PREDICTIVE MAINTENANCE
KW  - DIGITAL TWIN
KW  - SOFT SENSOR
KW  - SYSTEM
KW  - INSPECTION
KW  - ALGORITHM
KW  - BRIDGE
AB  - Tunnel leakage threatens the durability, safety, and serviceability of underground infrastructure, while conventional inspection methods remain limited by low efficiency, subjectivity, and poor capability for hidden seepage assessment. This review aims to clarify recent progress in artificial intelligence-based tunnel leakage detection and monitoring by examining sensing technologies, intelligent interpretation methods, and multimodal fusion strategies from a measurement-oriented perspective. Electromagnetic, optical, mechanical, vibration/ acoustic, environmental, wireless, self-powered, and multi-parameter sensing approaches are compared according to their measurable quantities, applicable tasks, advantages, limitations, and uncertainty sources. Deep learning methods are discussed in relation to leakage classification, detection, segmentation, localization, quantification, and early warning. The review indicates that vision- and LiDAR-assisted methods are comparatively mature for visible leakage recognition, boundary segmentation, and spatial localization, whereas fiberoptic, acoustic, thermal, and environmental sensing, supported by wireless and self-powered platforms, is better suited to hidden seepage inference, distributed monitoring, and long-term warning. Practical implementation is still constrained by limited annotated datasets, inconsistent calibration, environmental interference, weak cross-tunnel generalization, and insufficient evaluation of measurement robustness. To address these challenges, the Pluralistic Resonant Perception Theory (PRPT) is introduced as a conceptual framework for organizing heterogeneous sensing information, cross-modal interaction, spatiotemporal coordination, and semanticmechanistic coupling. Future research should emphasize standardized datasets, uncertainty-aware fusion, sensor calibration, edge deployment, digital twins, and field validation.
