KW  - Review
KW  - WORKERS-ON-FOOT
KW  - WORKPLACE SURVEILLANCE
KW  - MISS INTERACTIONS
KW  - CONSTRUCTION
KW  - AUTOMATION
KW  - EXCAVATOR
KW  - FRAMEWORK
KW  - JUSTICE
KW  - SYSTEM
KW  - MODEL
AB  - Heavy-duty equipment management increasingly relies on AI-enabled cyber-physical systems integrating sensors and digital twins to improve safety, efficiency, and sustainability. Despite technical advances, field deployment remains limited, suggesting adoption barriers driven more by socio-technical misalignment among intelligent systems, organizational processes, and human decision-making than by algorithmic capability. Accordingly, trust between humans and intelligent systems shapes whether automation yields operational gains. This review synthesizes 90 papers and four expert interviews across construction, logistics, and fleet management using bibliometric mapping and thematic synthesis. Although cross-domain in scope, the evidence base is constructiondominant and offers transferable insights for broader equipment management. Guided by socio-technical systems and relational trust-in-automation theories, this synthesis identified five socio-technical pillars: humantechnology interaction; workforce adaptation; data quality, integration, and scarcity; decision-making under uncertainty; and operational efficiency. Across these pillars, recurring constraints to AI-enabled system deployment include surveillance perceptions, trailer power/connectivity limits, and fragmented manufacturer portals, motivating a trust-centered framework.
