TI  - A fast-response framework for real-time 3D wind field reconstruction: Supporting urban air mobility applications in complex environments
T2  - SUSTAINABLE CITIES AND SOCIETY
LA  - English
KW  - Urban air mobility
KW  - Data fusion
KW  - Microscale wind field
KW  - Fast response
KW  - Diagnosis and evaluation
KW  - DISPERSION
KW  - SIMULATION
AB  - Urban Air Mobility (UAM) demands high-resolution, fast-response 3D wind diagnostics for flight safety and stability. However, current meteorological systems face severe bottlenecks: traditional numerical models are computationally too slow for timely hazard avoidance, while sparse single-point observations lack 3D spatial coverage. To address these gaps, this study proposes a real-time 3D wind field reconstruction framework designed for rapid cross-city deployment. It integrates Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) for vertical interpolation of observations, Space-Time Multiscale Analysis System (STMAS) for variational assimilation, and the QES-Winds microscale diagnostic model. Through one-way coupling between the mesoscale background field and the microscale model, the system generates a 5-m resolution 3D wind fields over a typical 4-km urban domain within 1 to 2 min. Validations in the Haixinsha low-altitude economy pilot zone demonstrate that the framework: (1) accurately resolves low-altitude flow field topologies, such as street canyon channeling and wake cavities; (2) matches independent observations with correlations of 0.699-0.901 and mean biases within-0.002 to 0.885 m/s; (3) proves that observational representativeness and building geometric accuracy are decisive for microscale flow reconstruction. Balancing computational efficiency and high-fidelity precision, the framework shows substantial potential for cross-city generalization, though its deployment remains subject to the complexity of local terrain and building geometries, as well as observation network density. Ultimately, it provides crucial data for dynamic route planning and offers theoretical support for low-altitude observational network siting and digital twin city construction.
