TI  - Real-time simulation for deep-sea mining system with sea trial validation
T2  - OCEAN ENGINEERING
LA  - English
KW  - Deep-sea mining
KW  - Dynamic response
KW  - Spatial configuration
KW  - Real-time simulation
KW  - Sea trial
AB  - This paper proposed a real-time simulation method for the deep-sea mining system to predict dynamic responses and the spatial configuration of the umbilical cable. The information of the marine environment and system motion were monitored by different sensors, such as USBLs (Ultra short baselines), ADCP, wave radar, tension sensor. The monitoring data were used to generate the real-time simulation model by a self-developed MATLAB-OrcaFlex interaction program. It achieved the shift from local monitoring data to global response prediction. The proposed method was verified during a 3300 m deep-sea mining sea trial. Compared with the top tension obtained by the tension sensor on the winch, the predicted result agreed well. Meanwhile, the error of the motion trajectories obtained by simulation and USBL was less than 2 m, which verified the reliability of the proposed method on the spatial configuration prediction. It was used to help engineers ensure the safety of the deep-sea mining system during the movement of the mining vehicle. The real-time simulation method provided technical support to the construction of the deep-sea mining digital twin in the near future. Besides, in the present paper, the analysis on undercurrents showed that undercurrents might make the cable much easier to touch seabed.
