TI  - RETRACTED: A comprehensive survey of battery energy in maritime transportation: Trends, challenges, and future perspectives (Retracted article. See vol. 358, 2026)
T2  - OCEAN ENGINEERING
LA  - English
KW  - Battery-powered ships
KW  - Hybrid systems
KW  - Renewable energy integration
KW  - Lithium-ion batteries (LIB)
KW  - Energy management systems (EMS)
KW  - COMPOSITES
AB  - The maritime industry is undergoing a significant transformation, driven by global environmental regulations and technological advancements. Battery energy has emerged as a promising alternative for ship propulsion, offering near-zero-emission operation and improved energy efficiency. This survey provides a comprehensive overview of battery energy applications in maritime ships, analyzing their technological advancements, challenges, and future perspectives. Both all-electric and hybrid systems are reviewed across various ship types, including ferries, container ships, and offshore support vessels. Key challenges, such as battery capacity, economic feasibility, and safety concerns, are discussed, along with recent innovations in lithium-ion, solid-state, and hybrid battery technologies. Furthermore, this survey highlights the integration of hybrid systems with renewable energy sources, such as hydrogen, solar, and wind power, which enhance ship efficiency and contribute to reducing carbon emissions. Future perspectives focus on the potential impact of policies and regulations, infrastructure development, and the application of battery energy across different ship types. Additionally, technological synergies, including autonomous shipping, digital twin (DT) technology, and energy management systems (EMS), are explored for their role in optimizing battery utilization.
