AB  - Hydrogen-powered electric vehicles have become a viable option for achieving sustainable, low-carbon transportation, especially for applications that require high utilization, quick refuelling, and long driving range. With a focus on proton exchange membrane fuel cells (FCs), hybrid powertrain architectures, and their integration into contemporary vehicle systems, this paper provides a thorough, up-to-date overview of hydrogen FC electric vehicle (HFCEV) technologies. FC technologies and system configurations, hydrogen generation pathways and related costs, and the operational features of FC battery and FC supercapacitor powertrains are all thoroughly reviewed in this study. In relation to hydrogen consumption, system efficiency, and durability, important elements of energy management and control approaches, such as rule-based, optimization-based, and artificial intelligence-driven strategies, are addressed. Additionally, the lifetime constraints and degradation mechanisms of automobile FC systems are also studied, highlighting the necessity of better materials and degradation-aware control algorithms to provide long-term operation. In contrast the international norms and safety considerations for other hydrogen storages and refuelling structured are also presented. A well-to-wheel and life cycle assessment studies are also considered for compare the environmental issues of the hydrogen based EVs with traditional and battery based EVs. In Indian circumstance, the assessment also observes modern controlling initiatives, deployment perspectives, and transportation applications across commercial, passenger, and heavyduty sectors. Subsequently, novel areas of study are described as critical facilitators of future hydrogen mobility, including low-platinum catalysts, advanced hydrogen storage technologies, digital twins, and artificial intelligence-based diagnostics. The results show that hydrogen-based electric vehicles can contribute significantly to sustainable transportation networks when supported by reliable infrastructure, renewable hydrogen generation, and ongoing improvements in FC durability and system integration.
