TI  - Serviceability assessment and structural response forecasting of near-coast utility tunnels under soft-soil conditions
T2  - STRUCTURES
LA  - English
KW  - Coastal urban utility tunnels
KW  - Soft-soil structural performance
KW  - Operational-phase monitoring
KW  - Hydrostatic leveling system (HLS)
KW  - Structural response prediction
AB  - Near-coast utility tunnels constructed in soft-soil environments are vulnerable to long-term settlement accumulation and structural response evolution, posing challenges for serviceability assessment and operational safety management. Existing studies generally focus on either deformation monitoring or structural response prediction, while integrated forecasting frameworks for heterogeneous monitoring data remain limited. This study proposes a dual-stream monitoring and predictive framework for operational-phase assessment of nearcoast utility tunnels. Hydrostatic leveling system (HLS) measurements are employed to characterize vertical deformation and are modeled using the AutoRegressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA) approach after stationarity diagnosis and differencing treatment. Meanwhile, strain-monitoring data are represented as a weighted sensor network and analyzed using a spatiotemporal graph neural network (STGNN) capable of capturing nonlinear temporal evolution and spatial coupling effects among monitoring locations. To ensure realistic operational evaluation, a rolling-origin forecasting strategy with missing-data masking and round-wise normalization is adopted. Results indicate that the ARIMA model achieved an RMSE of 0.62 mm and an R2 value of 0.93 for one-day deformation forecasting, demonstrating strong short-term predictive capability. For strainresponse prediction, the weighted STGNN achieved a 30-day forecasting RMSE of 0.025 mm and maintained a Pearson correlation coefficient exceeding 0.94. Comparative analyses show that the proposed multi-indicator STGNN outperformed single-indicator forecasting models by reducing prediction errors by approximately 15-20% for long-horizon forecasts. Ablation experiments further reveal that removing graph-based spatial coupling increased forecasting errors by 12.8%, confirming the importance of explicitly modeling inter-sensor dependencies. The proposed framework provides an effective and reproducible solution for structural prognosis, early warning, and maintenance planning of underground utility tunnels and offers a practical foundation for future digital-twin-driven infrastructure management systems.
